---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHead from '../components/PageHead.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import { href } from '../lib/url';
import { links, repoFile, disclaimer } from '../data/site';
---

<Base
  title="FAQ"
  description="Answers about Kat Linux: is it Arch, can you use the AUR, is it stable, how to install it, and how to report bugs."
>
  <PageHead eyebrow="FAQ" title="Questions people ask">
    Short answers, with links to the longer ones. Can't find yours? Ask on <a href={links.discord}>Discord</a>.
  </PageHead>

  <div class="section">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <nav class="rail" aria-label="FAQ topics">
        <p class="eyebrow">Topics</p>
        <ol class="toc">
          <li><a href="#about">About Kat Linux</a></li>
          <li><a href="#installing">Installing</a></li>
          <li><a href="#using">Using it</a></li>
          <li><a href="#project">The project</a></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <div class="faq">
        <section id="about" aria-labelledby="about-title">
          <h2 id="about-title">About Kat Linux</h2>

          <details id="is-it-arch">
            <summary>Is Kat Linux just Arch Linux?</summary>
            <div class="answer">
              <p>
                Underneath, yes. The ISO is built with archiso from Arch's official repositories, it uses pacman, and
                installing it runs archinstall, which sets up an Arch Linux system. What Kat Linux adds today is the
                live ISO itself: its branding, a ready-made Xfce desktop with LightDM, and a selection of apps and
                tools.
              </p>
              <p>A custom package manager and a full look of its own are on the <a href={href('/roadmap/')}>roadmap</a>.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details id="affiliated">
            <summary>Is it affiliated with Arch Linux?</summary>
            <div class="answer">
              <p>No. {disclaimer}</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details id="stable">
            <summary>Is it stable enough to use?</summary>
            <div class="answer">
              <p>
                Not yet. Only the base ISO is ticked off in the project's to-do list, and every push to the repository
                produces a new build. The packages themselves are Arch's, but the Kat Linux parts are changing quickly.
                Try it in a virtual machine or on a spare computer first.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details id="desktop">
            <summary>Which desktop does it use?</summary>
            <div class="answer">
              <p>
                The live ISO runs Xfce, started by LightDM with Slick Greeter. When you install, archinstall lets you
                pick any desktop profile it offers; choose Xfce4 to match the live session.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </section>

        <section id="installing" aria-labelledby="installing-title">
          <h2 id="installing-title">Installing</h2>

          <details id="installer">
            <summary>Is there a graphical installer?</summary>
            <div class="answer">
              <p>
                No. The ISO ships archinstall, which runs in a terminal and walks you through a menu. Calamares was
                added to the package list once, in September 2026, and removed again the same day. The <a
                  href={href('/install/')}>install guide</a
                > covers archinstall step by step.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details id="firmware">
            <summary>Does it boot on UEFI, BIOS and Secure Boot?</summary>
            <div class="answer">
              <p>
                UEFI and BIOS both work: syslinux handles BIOS and systemd-boot handles UEFI. Secure Boot isn't set up,
                so turn it off in your firmware settings if the stick won't start. Only 64-bit x86 (x86_64) PCs are
                supported.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details id="password">
            <summary>What's the password on the live ISO?</summary>
            <div class="answer">
              <p>
                There isn't one. LightDM logs the live user <code>archie</code> in automatically, and neither archie
                nor root has a password set on the ISO. You'll choose real passwords in archinstall.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details id="root">
            <summary>How do I get root on the live ISO?</summary>
            <div class="answer">
              <p>
                Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Alt</kbd> + <kbd>F1</kbd>. The first console is logged in as root
                automatically. <code>sudo</code> from the desktop won't work, because the profile doesn't give archie
                sudo rights yet.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </section>

        <section id="using" aria-labelledby="using-title">
          <h2 id="using-title">Using it</h2>

          <details id="aur">
            <summary>Can I use the AUR?</summary>
            <div class="answer">
              <p>
                Yes. An installed Kat Linux system is Arch underneath, so the <a href={links.aur}>Arch User Repository</a
                > works exactly as the Arch wiki describes. No AUR helper is installed for you. You can build packages
                by hand with makepkg:
              </p>
              <CodeBlock
                label="Build an AUR package by hand"
                code={`sudo pacman -S --needed base-devel git
git clone https://aur.archlinux.org/<package>.git
cd <package>
makepkg -si`}
              />
              <p class="muted">
                AUR packages are written by users, not checked by Arch or by us. Read the PKGBUILD before you build
                anything.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details id="updates">
            <summary>How do I update my system?</summary>
            <div class="answer">
              <p>The same way as on Arch. Kat Linux is rolling, so there are no version upgrades to wait for:</p>
              <CodeBlock label="Update" code="sudo pacman -Syu" />
            </div>
          </details>

          <details id="package-manager">
            <summary>Does Kat Linux have its own package manager?</summary>
            <div class="answer">
              <p>
                Not yet. A custom package manager is on the README's to-do list, but nothing has been built. For now
                it's pacman, like on Arch.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details id="multilib">
            <summary>Can I run 32-bit software?</summary>
            <div class="answer">
              <p>
                Yes, once you enable Arch's multilib repository. Pick it under optional repositories in archinstall,
                or uncomment the <code>[multilib]</code> section in <code>/etc/pacman.conf</code> afterwards.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </section>

        <section id="project" aria-labelledby="project-title">
          <h2 id="project-title">The project</h2>

          <details id="bugs">
            <summary>How do I report a bug?</summary>
            <div class="answer">
              <p>
                Open an issue at <a href={links.issues}>github.com/kat-linux/distro/issues</a>. Include the ISO file
                name (the date tells us which build), whether you booted with BIOS or UEFI, your hardware or VM, and
                what happened. The <a href={`${href('/community/')}#bugs-title`}>community page</a> has a checklist.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details id="updates-iso">
            <summary>How often is a new ISO built?</summary>
            <div class="answer">
              <p>
                On every push to the main branch. GitHub Actions builds it and attaches it to the release tagged
                <code>latest</code>, with the build date in the file name.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details id="help">
            <summary>Can I help?</summary>
            <div class="answer">
              <p>
                Please do. Join the <a href={links.discord}>Discord</a> and add the “Looking to Dev” role in
                onboarding. The <a href={href('/contribute/')}>contributing guide</a> explains how to build and test
                the ISO.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details id="license">
            <summary>What license is Kat Linux under?</summary>
            <div class="answer">
              <p>
                The distro repository is licensed under the <a href={repoFile('LICENSE')}>GNU General Public License
                v3</a>. The packages on the ISO keep their own licenses.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<script>
  // Open a question when the URL points at it, e.g. /faq/#aur
  const openFromHash = () => {
    const id = decodeURIComponent(location.hash.slice(1));
    if (!id) return;
    const el = document.getElementById(id);
    if (el instanceof HTMLDetailsElement) el.open = true;
  };
  openFromHash();
  window.addEventListener('hashchange', openFromHash);
</script>

<style>
  .faq {
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(2.5rem, 2rem + 2vw, 3.5rem);
    min-width: 0;
    max-width: 50rem;
  }

  .faq section {
    display: grid;
  }

  .faq h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    padding-bottom: 0.75rem;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--ink);
  }

  details {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  summary {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 1rem;
    padding: 1.05rem 0;
    cursor: pointer;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    line-height: 1.35;
    list-style: none;
  }

  summary::-webkit-details-marker {
    display: none;
  }

  summary::after {
    content: '';
    flex: none;
    width: 0.7rem;
    height: 0.7rem;
    border-right: 2.5px solid var(--nose);
    border-bottom: 2.5px solid var(--nose);
    rotate: 45deg;
    translate: 0 -0.2rem;
    transition: rotate 160ms ease;
  }

  details[open] > summary::after {
    rotate: -135deg;
    translate: 0 0.15rem;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    summary::after {
      transition: none;
    }
  }

  summary:hover {
    color: var(--nose);
  }

  .answer {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.85rem;
    padding-bottom: 1.5rem;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .answer .muted {
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }
</style>
